{"author":{"id":"8d2e0405f08ac6c481f912544d6d5c0f58b2dc14a7e51ad4d499ee7a34567943"},"ops":[{"type":6,"timestamp":1795543753,"nonce":"SUYeS70EFOtgqlBnZyEduM6cgyU=","target":"872441b866d9986cb3b0a6e55ad53c1e76d2f10aa5982aa8bfcdffd3e7ede2f6","message":"A rotating detonation engine tutorial was started and parked (2026-11-23): in the unrolled engine the one-step detonation wave decays instead of sustaining. Expected: a self-sustained wave near the Chapman-Jouguet speed 4.681, as in the planar channel.\n\n**Setup** The unrolled annulus is periodic in x; an injector plate feeds from a plenum through `q.bind(...).inside`; the exit is open. Chemistry is one-step Arrhenius, gamma 1.4, Q 10. The solver is `jno.fvm` with IMEX `ars222`, cfl 0.5. The scripts and data of the attempt are not in the repository.\n\n**Found** The numerics hold: a planar 2-D channel detonation reaches 94-96 % of the Chapman-Jouguet speed (4.681). In the engine the wave decays. Fresh-layer heights 3 and 8 both failed, with several activation-energy, pre-factor and plenum-pressure variants, and also when the run started from a fully formed detonation profile taken from the tube.\n\n**Suspected causes** The start-up state clashes with the injector (p* about 3.2 against the layer at p = 1), and the one-step heat-release tail is long against the layer height, so lateral expansion takes the energy. Neither is tested.\n\n**Where** The pieces it uses are `jno/_fvm/` (boundary `.inside` condition in `tests/test_fvm_inside.py`, the IMEX scheme in `jno/utils/solver/imex.py`); no jNO bug is known, the open question is physical.\n\n**Direction** Three ways on: two-step chemistry (induction, then heat release, the usual RDE model); more one-step tuning; or a smaller tutorial with the injector and a planar channel detonation. Close when one of them gives a tutorial that sustains its wave, or when the tutorial is dropped.","files":null}]}